CHAPTER III SCOPE

Article 3 (1) Every woman and child has the right to obtain protection from acts of violence.

2. Acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of acts of: a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. psychological violence; d. intimidation;
e. forced eviction;
f. deprivation of liberty; g. neglect; and h. exploitation and human trafficking.
3. Protection of women and children from acts of violence is the joint responsibility of the Regional Government, community, family, and parents.
4. Protection of women from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (2) includes: a. prevention of acts of violence against women and children; b. services for women and children victims of acts of violence; and c. empowerment of women and children victims of acts of violence.

CHAPTER IV PREVENTION

Article 4 (1) The Regional Government, Community, Family, and Parents are obliged to prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children to avoid the impact of situations and events.

2. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is also carried out against: a. acts of neglect; and b. indications or activities that contain the potential for human trafficking to occur.
3. Prevention efforts are carried out by taking into account the conditions, situations, problems and forms of handling that are appropriate and directed.

Article 5 (1) The Regional Government in accordance with its authority carries out early prevention efforts.

2. Early prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of: a. conducting data collection and assessment of conditions, fulfillment of rights and various aspects related to women and children in social life in the community;
b. mapping areas prone to acts of violence based on data collection and assessment as referred to in letter a and providing solutions in the form/manner capable of minimizing vulnerability;
c. increasing public legal awareness through the role of community institutions/customary institutions, interfaith communication forums to provide protection for women and children;
d. increasing the role of mass media in providing dissemination and information on the protection of women and children;
e. regular counseling or socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children;
f. opening channels for public complaints, and coordination channels from the village/sub-district, sub-district and Regional Government levels;
g. immediate handling of community reports.

CHAPTER IX GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 19 (1) The Regent shall conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of regional protection and empowerment of women and children.

2. The public is authorized to actively supervise the implementation of regional protection and empowerment of women and children.
3. The authority of the public as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be in the form of: a. providing input; b. asking questions and obtaining answers from responsible regional government officials;
c. requesting realization and concrete activities from those assigned tasks and authority;
d. other matters that are constructive and provide solutions.

3.6.text:
CHAPTER VII INSTITUTIONAL

Article 10 (1) In order to provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, neglect and human trafficking, the Regional Government establishes an Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A).

2. The Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) is positioned as an independent institution.

Article 11 (1) The Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) has the authority to organize the protection of women and children.

2. The duty of the Regional Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) is to implement protection for women and children in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the applicable laws and regulations.

Article 12 The membership of the Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) consists of elements of related agencies, vertical agencies, law enforcers, community organizations, non-governmental organizations, researchers, academics and religious, customary, community leaders and activists for the protection of women and children in the region.

Article 13 (1) The Regent establishes the Management of the Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A).

2. The establishment of Management as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Regent's Decree including the secretariat container for management and the provision of supporting facilities for the implementation of activities.

Article 14 (1) The Management of the Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment (P2TP2A) consists of leadership elements and members stipulated by Regent's Decree.

2. Leadership elements include: a. chairperson;
b. secretary; and c. treasurer.
3. Membership elements are divided into the form of: a. service field b. information field;
c. accompaniment field;
d. education and training field.

Article 15 (1) The Center for Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children in carrying out its functions builds a working network with: a. Police; b. Prosecutor's Office;
c. Court;
d. Witness and Victim Protection Agency; e. Hospital/Community Health Center;
f. Other institutions that have the same objectives.
2. In addition to building a working network as referred to in paragraph (1), cooperation can be carried out with: a. Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments; and d. Non-Governmental Institutions. e. International institutions whose existence is recognized.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

Article 16 (1) The Center for Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children establishes partnerships with the business world for support in organizing protection for women and children in the Regional area.

2. Partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1), are in the form of: a. information and opening of employment opportunities; b. provision of education and job training; c. provision of educational assistance for women and children victims of acts of violence; and

d. provision of independent business capital assistance.

Article 17 The Regent as the person responsible for the protection and empowerment of regional women and children has the authority to make changes to the management or dismissal of members if the existing management or members do not carry out their roles and functions.

CHAPTER VIII PUBLIC PARTICIPATION

Article 18 (1) The public may participate in providing protection and empowerment to women and children victims of violence, neglect and trafficking in persons.

2. Public participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. providing information to authorized officials or institutions;
b. assisting in the rescue of women and children;
c. providing basic and or specific needs assistance for women and children;
d. forming family partners or small units at the village/sub-district level that actively work together to protect the environment of women and children from acts of violence;
e. independently socializing the rights of women and children;
f. providing legal assistance and advocacy.
3. Public participation in providing protection and empowerment of women and children shall be carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

